  "prompt": "Which methods did Socrates employ to challenge the prevailing thoughts of his time?",
  "answer": "Socrates challenged the prevailing beliefs of his time primarily through **critical dialogue**, rather than lectures or written arguments. His methods included:\n\n- **Socratic questioning:** He asked probing questions about concepts such as justice, courage, virtue, and knowledge, pushing people to explain and justify their beliefs.\n- **Elenchus (cross-examination):** He examined the implications of someone’s answers, exposing contradictions and showing why apparently certain beliefs needed reconsideration.\n- **Professed ignorance:** Rather than claiming expertise, he presented himself as a learner—an approach often called *Socratic irony*—and questioned those who claimed to possess wisdom.\n- **Seeking clear definitions:** He challenged conventional examples and assumptions by asking what a virtue or concept actually meant in general.\n- **Creating productive uncertainty (*aporia*):** His conversations often ended without a definitive answer, encouraging people to recognize the limits of their knowledge and continue investigating.\n\nThrough these methods, Socrates encouraged people to rely on reasoned examination rather than tradition, social status, or popular opinion.",
